.TH REMSH 1N UniSoft
.SH NAME
remsh \- remote shell
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B remsh
host
[
.B \-l
username
] [
.B \-n
] command
.br
.B host
[
.B \-l
username
] [
.B \-n
] command
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Remsh\^
connects to the specified
.IR host ,
and executes the specified \fIcommand\fR.
.I Remsh\^
copies its standard input to the remote command, the standard
output of the remote command to its standard output, and the
standard error of the remote command to its standard error.
Interrupt, quit and terminate signals are propagated to the remote
command; \fIremsh\^\fP normally terminates when the remote command does.
.PP
The remote 
.I username\^
used is the same as your local username,
unless you specify a different remote name with the
.B \-l
option.
This remote name must be equivalent (in the sense of
.IR rlogin\^ (1))
to the originating account; no provision
is made for specifying a password with a command.
.PP
If you omit
.IR command ,
then instead of executing a single command, you will be logged in
on the remote host using
.IR rlogin\^ (1).
.PP
Shell metacharacters which are not quoted are interpreted
on the local machine, while quoted metacharacters are interpreted on
the remote machine.
Thus the command
.IP
remsh otherhost cat remotefile \|>>\| localfile
.PP
appends the remote file
"remotefile"
to the local file
"localfile",
while
.IP
remsh otherhost cat remotefile \|">>"\| otherremotefile
.PP
appends
"remotefile"
to
"otherremotefile".
.PP
Host names are given in the file 
.BR /etc/hosts .
Each host
has one standard name (the first name given in the file), which
is rather long and unambiguous, and optionally one or more nicknames.
The host names for local machines 
maybe linked to the 
.I remsh\^
command in some convenient place,
normally in the directory
.BR /usr/host .
If this directory is in one's search path, then the 
.I remsh\^
can be omitted.
.SH FILES
/etc/hosts
.br
/usr/hosts/\(**
.br
/etc/remsh
.SH SEE ALSO
rlogin(1N).
.SH BUGS
If you are using
.IR csh\^ (1)
and put a
.IR remsh\^ (1)
in the background without redirecting its input
away from the terminal, it will block even if no reads
are posted by the remote command.  If no input is desired,
you should redirect the input of
.I remsh\^
to 
.B /dev/null
using the
.B \-n
option.
.PP
You cannot run an interactive command
(like
.IR vi\^ (1));
use
.IR rlogin\^ (1).
.PP
Stop signals stop the local 
.I remsh\^
process only; this is arguably
wrong, but currently hard to fix for reasons too complicated to
explain here.
.PP
This command is provisional and may change in 
future releases.
.\"N	 @(#)remsh.1N	 5.2 of 7/12/84
